ABOUT
-----
Sake is a build automation tool written in GNU Guile Scheme, similar in
function to GNU Make.

In Sake, a "build" process consists of creating or updating target files
when their prerequisite files have changed. Sake reads a configuration file
named sakefile.scm, which defines targets, dependencies, and build
commands using Guile S-expressions.

For each rule, Sake inspects the filesystem modification times (mtime):

1. If a target file does not exist, Sake runs the specified command.

2. If any dependency is newer than the target file, Sake runs the command.

3. If the target file is up to date relative to all dependencies, the
command is skipped.

Sake recursively resolves dependency chains before executing commands.

Sake is free software, distributed under the terms of the GNU General 
Public License version 3 or (at your option) any later version (GPLv3+).

USAGE EXAMPLE
-------------
A simple sakefile.scm for compiling a C program:

(target "main"
#:deps '("main.c" "utils.c")
#:cmd "gcc -o main main.c utils.c")

(target "clean"
#:cmd "rm -f main")

Run Sake to build the default (first) target:
sake

Or build a specific target:
sake clean

DEPENDENCIES
------------
To run and build Sake, you need:
  - GNU Guile 3.0 or later
  - Standard POSIX utilities (sh, coreutils)


QUICK START
-----------
Bootstrap the project:
   ./bootstrap.sh

DOCUMENTATION
-------------
Complete documentation for Sake is available in Texinfo format.
To read the manual locally:

     info doc/sake.info

Or build the documentation from source:

     makeinfo doc/sake.texi


